<BaseLayout title="Key Rotation" description="RCAN §8.6 — JWKS key lifecycle, KEY_ROTATION message type, grace period overlap, and key_id on signed messages.">
  <nav class="doc-nav">
    <a href="/docs/">← Documentation</a>
  </nav>

  <article class="doc-article">
    <h1>§8.6 — Key Lifecycle and Rotation <span class="badge-new">v1.5</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">Earlier <a href="https://docs.rcan.dev/spec/">RCAN protocol</a> drafts used a single, non-expiring signing key per operator. Subsequent revisions add JWKS-based key management with expiry, rotation, and a grace period so production fleets can rotate keys without dropping active sessions.</p>

    <section>
      <h2>JWKS Endpoint</h2>
      <p>Robots and operators SHOULD maintain a JSON Web Key Set (JWKS) at:</p>
      <pre><code>/.well-known/rcan-keys.json</code></pre>
      <p>The registry proxies each robot's JWKS at:</p>
      <pre><code>GET /api/v1/robots/&#123;rrn&#125;/.well-known/rcan-keys.json</code></pre>

      <h3>JWKS Format</h3>
      <pre><code>&#123;
  "keys": [
    &#123;
      "kty":        "OKP",
      "crv":        "Ed25519",
      "kid":        "rcan-key-2026-03",
      "use":        "sig",
      "key_ops":    ["verify"],
      "x":          "base64url-encoded-public-key",
      "exp":        1782000000,       // REQUIRED: max 365 days from creation
      "iat":        1750000000,       // Key creation time
      "revoked_at": null,             // Set when key is compromised
      "domain_proof": "base64url-ed25519-signature"   // OPTIONAL, see Domain Proof below
    &#125;,
    &#123;
      "kty":        "OKP",
      "crv":        "Ed25519",
      "kid":        "rcan-key-2025-03",
      "use":        "sig",
      "key_ops":    ["verify"],
      "x":          "base64url-encoded-old-public-key",
      "exp":        1750000000,       // Past expiry
      "iat":        1718000000,
      "revoked_at": null
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Domain Proof (optional)</h2>
      <p>A JWKS is public, so anyone can copy another operator's public key into their own <code>rcan-keys.json</code>. Where DNSSEC <code>_rcan</code> TXT records are not in use, nothing ties a key to the domain that serves it. An Ed25519 key MAY carry <code>domain_proof</code>: an Ed25519 signature by that key, base64url without padding, over the UTF-8 bytes of</p>
      <pre><code>rcan-keys domain-proof/1 domain=&lt;host&gt; kid=&lt;thumbprint&gt;</code></pre>
      <p>where <code>&lt;host&gt;</code> is the lower-case host name serving the file and <code>&lt;thumbprint&gt;</code> is the RFC 7638 JWK thumbprint of the key (SHA-256 over <code>&#123;"crv","kty","x"&#125;</code>, base64url, 43 characters). The thumbprint is used rather than the <code>kid</code> member so the proof does not depend on how the operator names the key.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A verifier that relies on the binding between a key and a domain MUST check <code>domain_proof</code> with the key itself, and MUST treat a key without a valid proof as published by that domain but not bound to it.</li>
        <li>Readers that do not use the binding ignore the member (RFC 7517 §4). Existing key sets remain valid.</li>
        <li>The proof does not cover <code>iat</code>, <code>exp</code> or <code>revoked_at</code>; those stay under the control of whoever serves the file, as today.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A key set MAY also carry Bounded Embodiment <code>key-0.1</code> entries (<code>key_version</code>, <code>kid</code>, <code>public_jwk</code>, <code>lab</code>, <code>contact</code>, <code>added</code>, <code>revoked</code>), whose <code>kid</code> is already the RFC 7638 thumbprint. Readers map <code>added</code> to <code>iat</code> and <code>revoked</code> to <code>revoked_at</code>; such entries have no <code>exp</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>key_id on Signed Messages</h2>
      <p>All signed RCAN messages MUST include a <code>key_id</code> field in the envelope identifying which key was used to sign:</p>
      <pre><code>&#123;
  "id":        "uuid-v4",
  "type":      1,
  "key_id":    "rcan-key-2026-03",  // kid of the signing key
  "signature": "ed25519:...",
  ...
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>Receivers MUST look up the signing key by <code>key_id</code> in the sender's JWKS before verifying the signature. If the <code>key_id</code> is not found in the JWKS, reject with <code>KEY_NOT_FOUND</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Key Rotation Procedure</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Generate</strong> a new Ed25519 key pair</li>
        <li><strong>Add</strong> the new public key to JWKS with a new <code>kid</code></li>
        <li><strong>Start signing</strong> new messages with the new key (update <code>key_id</code>)</li>
        <li><strong>Wait</strong> for the grace period (<code>overlap_s</code>, default 3600s = 1 hour)</li>
        <li><strong>Set <code>exp</code></strong> on the old key to <code>now()</code> (not a future time)</li>
        <li><strong>Broadcast</strong> KEY_ROTATION message (type 27) to peers</li>
        <li><strong>Retain</strong> old key in JWKS for at least <code>replay_window_s * 2</code> after expiry for in-flight validation</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout callout-warn">
        <strong>Grace period is mandatory:</strong> During the overlap window, both the old and new keys are valid. This prevents disruption to active sessions that have cached the old key. Do not expire the old key before <code>overlap_s</code> has elapsed.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>KEY_ROTATION Message (Type 27)</h2>
      <pre><code>&#123;
  "id":           "uuid-v4",
  "type":         27,
  "source":       "rcan://rcan.dev/org/robot/v1/unit-001",
  "rcan_version": "3.2",
  "qos":          1,
  "payload": &#123;
    "new_kid":    "rcan-key-2026-03",
    "old_kid":    "rcan-key-2025-03",
    "overlap_s":  3600,              // Grace period in seconds
    "jwks_url":   "/.well-known/rcan-keys.json"
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>Peers receiving KEY_ROTATION MUST refresh their cached JWKS for the sender within 60 seconds.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Key Validity Rules</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Key State</th><th>Verification</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Active: <code>iat ≤ now &lt; exp</code>, <code>revoked_at: null</code></td>
            <td>✅ Accept</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Grace period: <code>now ≤ exp + (replay_window_s * 2)</code>, <code>revoked_at: null</code></td>
            <td>✅ Accept for in-flight messages only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Expired: <code>now > exp + (replay_window_s * 2)</code></td>
            <td>❌ Reject with <code>KEY_EXPIRED</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Revoked: <code>revoked_at != null</code></td>
            <td>❌ Reject immediately with <code>KEY_REVOKED</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Not in JWKS</td>
            <td>❌ Reject with <code>KEY_NOT_FOUND</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p><strong>Max key validity:</strong> 365 days. Compliance frameworks (SOC 2, ISO 27001) typically require ≤90-day rotation; set <code>exp</code> accordingly.</p>

      <h3>Signed Records</h3>
      <p>The rules above apply to live messages, checked against the current time. Some signed artifacts are records kept for years: audit bundles, Bounded Embodiment test reports, and records kept with an EU 2023/1230 technical file for 10 years. For a signed record that carries its own signing time <code>signed_at</code>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A verifier SHOULD accept the key when <code>iat ≤ signed_at ≤ exp</code> and either <code>revoked_at</code> is null or <code>signed_at &lt; revoked_at</code>, even if the key has since expired.</li>
        <li>A verifier MUST reject the key for a record whose <code>signed_at</code> is at or after <code>revoked_at</code>.</li>
        <li>Operators SHOULD keep expired keys in <code>rcan-keys.json</code>, with <code>exp</code> in the past, for as long as records signed with them must be checkable, and MUST NOT remove a key's <code>revoked_at</code> once set. Verifiers that have seen a revocation SHOULD keep it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A record's own signing time is only as trustworthy as its signer; where that matters, anchor records in an append-only log with an independent timestamp.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Registry Key History</h2>
      <p>The registry MUST store key history (not just the current key) for each registered robot. This enables:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Validation of messages signed with recently-rotated keys</li>
        <li>Audit trail verification against historical signatures</li>
        <li>Key compromise investigation</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Registry endpoint: <code>GET /api/v1/robots/&#123;rrn&#125;/public-key</code> returns the current active key. The JWKS endpoint returns the full history.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Configuration</h2>
      <pre><code>security:
  key_rotation:
    overlap_s: 3600           # Grace period: both keys valid (default 1h)
    max_validity_days: 365    # Hard maximum key lifetime
    auto_rotate: false        # Enable automatic rotation (requires key management setup)</code></pre>
    </section>

